{
 "id": "lung_alveoli",
 "title": "Lung: alveoli and capillaries",
 "node": "lung_alveoli_scene",
 "tag": "Inside the lung",
 "cell": "animal",
 "bg": "#f3efe6",
 "center": [
  0,
  -41,
  0
 ],
 "D_um": 1250,
 "blurb": "A slice through three air sacs (alveoli) of a lung and a small airway. The thin wall between air and blood holds a capillary with red cells in single file. Zoom into the wall to reach the barrier, drawn 0.6 micrometres thick. Click a part.",
 "note": "A schematic, not a micrograph: three alveoli packed together, cut across, with a small bronchiole at the left edge, drawn there for scale only; in life it reaches the sacs through respiratory bronchioles and alveolar ducts, not directly. REAL sizes: the alveolus (about 200 micrometres across), the blood-air barrier (0.6 micrometres, drawn as a sheet of that thickness on each side of each capillary: 0.575 for the capillary lining and basement membrane plus 0.025 for the flat type I cell), the red cell diameter (7.5 micrometres) and the bronchiole (about 0.5 mm across, the upper limit given for a terminal bronchiole). The alveolar and barrier figures are human; no dog-specific size could be confirmed (a secondary summary of Siegwart et al. 1971 suggests a dog harmonic mean near 0.5 micrometres, not opened), so the dog lung is drawn with the same numbers and may differ. The 0.6 micrometre figure is drawn as a round value within the 0.5 to 0.6 range given by the sources opened (0.5 in OpenStax, 0.57 harmonic mean in rat, less than 1 in Knudsen 2023); a human harmonic mean of 0.6 was not confirmed from an opened source. A harmonic mean is the thickness that matters for diffusion; the average thickness is larger and the thinnest parts about 0.2-0.3 micrometres. ILLUSTRATIVE: the wall thickness (10), the capillary width (8), the red cell thickness (2), the type II cell size (10), the macrophage size (20), the bronchiole wall and lining thickness, the number and placement of cells, and the straight, flat capillaries (real ones form a net across the wall). NOT DRAWN: the surfactant film, the pores between neighbouring sacs, elastic fibres and the cells of the connective tissue in the wall. The sacs are drawn round and shallow (scene depth about 60 micrometres); real ones are many-sided. Birds have no alveoli: their lungs exchange gas in air capillaries a few micrometres wide, so this scene is for the lungs of mammals such as people and dogs, not for birds.",
